// Outer product (rank-1 update / BLAS-2 `ger`) — the conceptual
// OPPOSITE of a reduction.
//
// Two input vectors a (length M, the "column" vector) and b (length N,
// the "row" vector) produce a 2D matrix c with
//
//     c[i][j] = a[i] * b[j]
//
// There is NO contraction and NO reduction: every output element is an
// independent product of one a element and one b element. The rank
// EXPANDS (1D x 1D -> 2D) rather than contracting. That single property
// is what distinguishes this example from the neighbours that look
// superficially similar:
//
//   - 03-reduction / 23-dot-product CONTRACT (sum_k ...): they collapse
//     an axis. This example does the opposite — it introduces a new
//     output axis for every input axis.
//   - 07-matmul CONTRACTS along k: c[i][j] = sum_k A[i][k]*B[k][j]. The
//     outer product is the degenerate "K=1, no accumulate" case — the
//     rank-1 building block matmul sums over. Here there is no `k` loop
//     and no accumulator at all.
//   - 15-transpose is a PERMUTATION of one input (rank-preserving); the
//     outer product reads two distinct 1D inputs and writes a 2D
//     output it builds from scratch.
//   - 01-elementwise-add is a per-element MAP over two EQUAL-length 1D
//     arrays with a SHARED index (c[i] = a[i]+b[i]). The outer product
//     reads a and b with DIFFERENT index variables (a[i], b[j]) so the
//     output is the full Cartesian M x N grid, not a single 1D result.
//
// RECTANGULAR dims (M != N) are deliberate: c is non-square (8 x 16), so
// the rank expansion is unmistakable and the example can never be
// mistaken for a 2D-elementwise op over equal shapes.
//
// What this example demonstrates:
//   - Reading TWO different 1D arrays with TWO different single index
//     variables in one pure kernel call: `mul(a[i], b[j])`. The outer
//     loop variable `i` indexes a; the inner loop variable `j` indexes
//     b. This is a strict SUBSET of 07-matmul's mixed-index reads
//     `madd(c[i][j], a[i][k], b[k][j])` (which mixes i/k and k/j in 2D
//     index expressions and accumulates); here each read uses exactly
//     one loop variable into a 1D array, and there is no accumulate.
//   - A 2D row-major output `c : i32[M][N]` written once per (i, j)
//     under single-assignment (PRD §6.2.1: the rule is on the data
//     SYMBOL — one dataflow statement assigns `c` — not on every loop
//     iteration). Unlike 07-matmul, `c` is NOT pre-initialised to an
//     accumulator identity because there is no fold: each c[i][j] is a
//     single straight assignment from one product.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate:
//   - Reduction / accumulation. There is no `sum_k`, no accumulator,
//     no tree combine. That is the whole point — it is the rank
//     EXPANSION counterpart to 03/23.
//   - Distributed placement across compute workers. The naive schedule
//     places everything on `host`; the cross-backend differential is
//     the acceptance signal. A distributed variant would partition the
//     `i` axis (each worker owns a strip of rows of c, needing the
//     whole of b), but that is out of scope here.
//   - Float arithmetic. PRD §10.1 demands bit-identity; integer only.
//
// Numeric choice & determinism (PRD §10.1): i32 throughout, with
// `wrapping_mul` in the kernel. Integer multiply is bit-deterministic
// by language definition. Crucially, because there is NO reduction,
// every output element c[i][j] is a single independent product — the
// result is order-independent BY CONSTRUCTION (no associativity or
// commutativity argument is even needed; there is nothing to reorder).
// So bit-identity across all backends and all schedules is automatic.
// The committed fixture (see reference/) keeps every product tiny, well
// inside the i32 range, but the `wrapping_mul` spelling documents the
// overflow contract defensively.
//
// Row-major layout: `c : i32[M][N]` is a single flat Vec<i32> of length
// M*N laid out row-major — c[i][j] at flat index i*N + j. The codegen
// resolves the N stride at compile time from the data symbol's shape
// (identical convention to 07-matmul's `c[i][j] -> c[i*N + j]`).

const M : usize = 8;     // length of column vector a (rows of c)
const N : usize = 16;    // length of row vector   b (cols of c)


// Two input vectors and the 2D outer-product matrix. Each `data`
// symbol is assigned by exactly one dataflow statement (PRD §6.2.1):
// `a` and `b` are whole-array assigned by their loaders; `c` is
// indexed-assigned once per (i, j) by the single `mul` statement.
data a : i32[M];         // column vector (length M)
data b : i32[N];         // row vector    (length N)
data c : i32[M][N];      // outer product: c[i][j] = a[i] * b[j]


// Pure compute kernel: scalar multiply. `mul(x, y) = x * y`. Scalar
// (i32, i32) -> i32 so the contract pass (TASK-0012, scalar-only
// matching at M1) PASSES on it directly. Pure: reorderable,
// deduplicable, eliminable.
kernel mul : (i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved within a basic block,
// never duplicated. Aggregate-typed in Nuc; on the Rust side they use
// `Vec<i32>` (see kernels.rs and the README "Why Vec<i32>" note).
//   load_a reads a from input.bin positions [0..M);
//   load_b reads b from positions [M..M+N) (single-file two-input
//   convention, same as 01/23).
kernel load_a      : ()          -> i32[M]    effectful;
kernel load_b      : ()          -> i32[N]    effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[M][N]) -> ()        effectful;


// Dataflow: load both vectors, then fire `mul` over the full M x N grid
// with DISTINCT index variables (a[i] with the outer var, b[j] with the
// inner var), writing each c[i][j] exactly once. No reduction.
a <-- load_a();
b <-- load_b();

for i : 0 .. M {
    for j : 0 .. N {
        c[i][j] <-- mul(a[i], b[j]);   // outer product: a[i] (outer) * b[j] (inner)
    }
}

save_output(c);
